WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.936
GRADY POWELL: Bladesmiths,
congratulations.

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:04.170
You're moving forward in
the third and final round

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.338
of our competition
where we're sending you

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.006
back to your home
forges to build

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:08.608
an iconic weapon from history.

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:10.510
Now I'm going to apologize
in advance because I

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:11.877
didn't choose this blade.

00:00:11.977 --> 00:00:12.845
That was up to J.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.380
[uptempo music]

00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:15.948
 Bladesmiths, you didn't think
I was going to start taking

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:17.517
it easy on you now, did you?

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:18.784
 No.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:20.120
 I want you to build this.

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.460
Mindanao Kris.

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:31.230
 [sighs]

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.600
The Mindanao Kris traces its
origin back to the 14th century

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:38.838
Mindanao, the second largest
island in the Philippines.

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.340
This double-edged
sword is defined

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:42.908
by its distinct waves
designed to inflict

00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:44.576
wide, deadly slashes in combat.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:45.945
[splattering]

00:00:46.011 --> 00:00:47.347
The amount of waves in
the warrior's weapon

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:50.015
also indicates their
status within the tribe.

00:00:50.082 --> 00:00:52.184
The more waves, the
higher the rank.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:54.320
The Kris was the most
common moro sword

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.789
and many can be seen today
in museums across the world,

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:59.558
including the Metropolitan
Museum of Art in New York City.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:01.260
GRADY POWELL: You'll have
four days at your home

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:02.662
forge to build your blades.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.363
Good luck, gentlemen,
when we see you then.

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:05.331
 All right.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:05.864
Good luck.

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:06.899
 Good luck.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:09.669 line:14%
[uptempo music]

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:12.305 line:8%
JOHN BLANKMAN: I'm John
Blankman from Avon, Utah.

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:14.540 line:14%
I'm a fire sprinkler contractor.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.143
I like bladesmithing because,
at this point in my life,

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.479
I'm stuck behind a desk
most of the time and this

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:22.248
allows me to get back out
and do things with my hands

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:25.184
and it's just fulfilling.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:26.686
And they're throwing
curves at us.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.054
Curves are not my thing.

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:30.055
I haven't done them, but
I am pretty confident I

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:31.624
can get this thing done.

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:33.259
So we're going to start
getting the curves

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:36.329
into the blade itself,
then taking a lot of weight

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:37.563
off of it.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.032
For now I set up some jigs
to get these curves bent

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.234
into the blade.

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.435
So at any given
moment when you're

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:44.337
putting these radical
of a bend in it,

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:49.242
your steel could rip
and have a tear in it.

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:50.643
You got to kind of
cross your fingers

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.011
that you're doing it right.

00:01:52.077 --> 00:01:53.546
That should work.

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.080
So the blade's come
along really nice.

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:58.617
It's in pretty good shape
where I want it to be.

00:01:58.684 --> 00:01:59.852
Oh, crap.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.888
Got a little bit
of a crack here.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:04.456
Seriously?

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:07.727
If I can't get this crack
ground out, then this is junk.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:08.595
OK.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.835
Finally, I get to the bottom
of it and it is still saveable

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:17.069
and I don't have to start over.

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:18.471
Things are going
along pretty good.

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.239
Trying to get the blade
polished up a little bit,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.975
get the handle put onto
it, got to get it etched,

00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:24.943
and finish it up.

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:26.145
Looking good.

00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:27.413
All right, got it
all sharpened up.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.984
Let's go out and give
her a quick test.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:33.052
I'm feeling great about
what I've accomplished.

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:34.821
I do you believe
that's all I can do.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.091 line:8%
DALE BRANNIGAN:
I'm Dale Brannigan

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.593 line:14%
from Shelby, North Carolina.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.429
I design technology
systems for banks

00:02:43.529 --> 00:02:44.997
and financial institutions.

00:02:45.063 --> 00:02:47.333
I like forging for the escape.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.935
I get to hit things with
hammers and play with fire.

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:50.836
[laughs]

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:52.638
Steel's moving slow.

00:02:52.738 --> 00:02:53.840
Press is a little weak.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.174
Keeps bending it
all over the place,

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:59.111
so I have to keep
straightening it out.

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:00.313
We're getting there.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:01.814
That's starting to
look like a Kris.

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:04.817
Handle material is pretty
close to shape already.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.152
All right.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:08.087
The handle is glued
up, the epoxy is set.

00:03:08.153 --> 00:03:09.422
Doesn't look bad.

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:11.524
Now I'm going to wrap
the handle with hemp.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:13.826
It's not a requirement,
but it'll give a good grip

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:14.960
and it looks kind of cool.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:16.295
I have a completed weapon.

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:19.097
Just to turn in something
complete at the end

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:21.233
of these rounds
is kind of a feat,

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:24.336
so I'm super happy
that I'm done.

00:03:24.403 --> 00:03:27.006
 Bladesmiths, welcome
to the kill test.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.876
To find out what kind of lethal
damage your weapon will do,

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:32.678
I will take your Kris and
deliver some lethal blows

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.347
on this pig carcass.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:35.214
John, you're first.

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:36.015
Are you ready for this?

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:36.949
 You bet.

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:37.883
 All right, let's do this.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:41.588
[building music]

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:48.861
[thudding]

00:03:59.071 --> 00:03:59.839
 Sweet.

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:06.212
All right, John.

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:07.613
Let's talk about your
Mindanao Kris here.

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:10.182
I really appreciate that
you have this large flare

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:14.020
because it's a very
forward heavy blade,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.357
but because of that weight and
a very sharp edge you have here,

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:21.527
these are very, very deep
cuts on this pig carcass.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:22.061
It'll kill.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:22.828
 [laughs]

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.163
Cool.

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:25.398
 Dale, your turn,
so are you ready?

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:26.699
 That's what I'm here for.

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.200
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, let's do this.

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:31.871
[heavy metal music]

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:38.811
[thudding]

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:46.686
 Neat, man.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:47.687
 Good job.

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:50.456
[suspenseful music]

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:51.991
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, Dale, let's talk

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:54.126
about your Mindanao Kris.

00:04:54.192 --> 00:04:56.362
First up, it's a lot lighter.

00:04:56.429 --> 00:04:59.065
Now that allows
me to use velocity

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:01.834
to cut down a pig like this
with the same amount of strikes.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:07.072
The edge will cut deeply and,
overall sir, your weapon it

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:08.073
will kill.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:08.975
 Awesome.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:15.248
 Bladesmiths, welcome
to our strength test.

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:18.483
One of my personal favorites,
the ice block chop.

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:20.252
I'm going to take
your Kris swords

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.088
and try to free those masks.

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:23.889
We're going to have some fun.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:24.723
John, you're up first.

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:25.491
You ready to go?

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:26.125
 I am.

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:27.193
Go make snow cones.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:28.728
 Well, we could do that.

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:32.332
[heavy metal music]

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:43.710
[grunting]

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:55.921
[laughs]

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:56.822
 Damn.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.125
[suspenseful music]

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:00.325
Nice work, John.

00:06:00.425 --> 00:06:02.894
Love the Damascus.

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:04.464
Very heavy.

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:08.067
It tears through the eyes, but
if the ice block was any bigger

00:06:08.133 --> 00:06:09.969
it'd be tearing through me, too.

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:14.940
And you got a bit of a
warp, but still razor sharp.

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:15.807
Good job.

00:06:15.874 --> 00:06:16.942
 Thanks.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:18.577
 Dale, you ready to go?

00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:20.579
 If anybody's going to break my
sword it might as well be you.

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:21.913
 Good.
I appreciate that.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:22.782
Let's do it.

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:25.785
[heavy metal music]

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:33.159
[grunting]

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:48.674
[suspenseful music]

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:50.109
Dale, nice job.

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:55.881
The only complaint I have
is your wrap here is loose,

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:59.785
but everything's still
tight, your edge is good.

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:00.352
Nice job.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:04.690
 Thanks, J.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.259
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, bladesmiths.

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:09.862
This is the sharpness
test, the palm slice.

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:11.297
To find out how sharp
your weapons are,

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:12.865
I'm going to take
your Mindanao Krises

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:16.135
and try to cut
through these palms.

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:17.003
John you are first.

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:17.703
You ready for this?

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:18.404
 I am.

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:21.774
[suspenseful music]

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:27.713
[heavy metal music]

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:34.587
 Nice.

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:39.124
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, John, let's talk

00:07:39.224 --> 00:07:40.359
about your weapon here.

00:07:40.459 --> 00:07:43.662
First up, your edge
is still very sharp.

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:47.032
It's just that the weight of
this has me holding on to it

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:49.235
because I don't want
this to be flying around.

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:52.171
But for this particular
test, it will cut.

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:53.406
 Thank you.

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:54.873
 Dale, your turn,
so are you ready?

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:55.608
 Slice it up.

00:07:55.675 --> 00:07:56.542
 All right, will do.

00:07:56.609 --> 00:08:00.746
[suspenseful music]

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:07.086
[heavy metal music]

00:08:15.794 --> 00:08:18.397
All right, Dale, let's talk
about your Mindanao Kris here.

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:20.966
It's thin and razor sharp.

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:24.536
Definitely the type of edge you
want in cutting these brushes.

00:08:24.636 --> 00:08:27.339
And overall, sir, your
edge, it will cut.

00:08:27.406 --> 00:08:28.174
 Thanks, Doug.

00:08:30.475 --> 00:08:32.011
GRADY POWELL: Well,
bladesmiths, before we

00:08:32.077 --> 00:08:33.746
get into the hard part, I want
to say it's been a pleasure

00:08:33.812 --> 00:08:35.281
watching both of you guys work.

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:37.983
There's been a lot of twists
and turns and J's challenge,

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:42.454
but this is a competition and
there's only one champion.

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:44.223
The "Forged in Fire"
champion today is--

00:08:44.323 --> 00:08:47.326
[suspenseful music]

00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:48.059
--Dale.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.728
Congratulations.

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:51.830
John, you did a fantastic job,
but, unfortunately, your Kris

00:08:51.931 --> 00:08:53.332
isn't going to end
up on the wall.

00:08:53.399 --> 00:08:54.600
Your time in this
competition has ended.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.035
I'm going to have
to ask you to please

00:08:56.102 --> 00:08:57.035
step off the forge floor.

00:08:57.135 --> 00:08:58.170
 All right.

00:08:58.271 --> 00:08:59.738
It's been a great
experience, man.

00:08:59.838 --> 00:09:01.073
GRADY POWELL: Thank you, John.
J. NEILSON: Thank you, John.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.140
DOUG MARCAIDA: Thank you, John.
- Appreciate it.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.675
 Great job, John.

00:09:03.742 --> 00:09:04.543
JOHN BLANKMAN: This whole
journey with "Forged in Fire"

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:05.244
has been incredible.

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:06.612
 [laughs]

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:07.946
GRADY POWELL: Well, Dale,
congratulations, man.

00:09:08.013 --> 00:09:09.682
That means you are the
newest "Forged in Fire"

00:09:09.748 --> 00:09:11.217
champion you just won
yourself a check for $10,000.

00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:12.050
Well done.

00:09:12.117 --> 00:09:12.985
[applause]

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:14.253
 I'm blown away.

00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:15.421
Super proud.

00:09:15.488 --> 00:09:16.955
Literally the day
before I started

00:09:17.055 --> 00:09:18.991
that was five years to the day
that I lit my first forge--

00:09:19.091 --> 00:09:20.526
 No kidding.
DALE BRANNIGAN: --so--

00:09:20.626 --> 00:09:22.094
DOUG MARCAIDA: Who knew
five years later you'd

00:09:22.161 --> 00:09:23.662
be a "Forged in Fire" champ?

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:25.631
 Everybody who knows
that you make knives asks,

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:27.266
when are you going to
be on "Forged in Fire?"

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:28.801
Now I can tell
them, not only have

00:09:28.901 --> 00:09:32.370
I been on "Forged in Fire," but
I won a J. Nielsen challenge.

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:35.975
[uptempo music]

